+# Rule Engine
+
+A small, dependency-free Python engine that evaluates a **tree of rules**
+(nested `AND`/`OR`/`NOT` groups + leaf conditions) against input data coming
+from **document extraction fields** and **application data fields**.
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+rule-engine/
+├── pyproject.toml           # packaging metadata + console scripts
+├── src/rule_engine/         # the installable library
+│   ├── __init__.py          # public API
+│   ├── operators.py         # operator registry (not_empty, equals, before, ...)
+│   ├── context.py           # resolves operand values from input data
+│   ├── engine.py            # recursive tree evaluation + evaluation modes
+│   ├── result.py            # RuleResult / EvaluationReport (+ executed_rules)
+│   ├── repository.py        # rule sets indexed by exception_code
+│   ├── service.py           # RuleEngineService — the facade the app calls
+│   └── cli.py               # console entry points
+├── rules/                   # rule set definitions (one per exception code)
+├── data/                    # sample payloads / inputs
+├── main.py                  # shim -> rule-engine-single
+└── run_service.py           # shim -> rule-engine
+```
+
+## Setup on Windows
+
+Prerequisites: **Python 3.12.0** and **Node 20.9** (only needed for the UI).
+
+- Python — https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-3120/
+  (during install, tick **"Add python.exe to PATH"**).
+- Node — https://nodejs.org/download/release/v20.9.0/ (or `nvm-windows`).
+
+### Backend (rule engine + REST API)
+
+Open **PowerShell** in the project root (`rule-engine\`) and run:
+
+```powershell
+# 1. Confirm the right Python
+py -3.12 --version                 # -> Python 3.12.0
+
+# 2. Create and activate a virtual environment
+py -3.12 -m venv .venv
+.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1       # prompt now shows (.venv)
+
+# 3. Install the package with the API extra
+python -m pip install --upgrade pip
+python -m pip install -e ".[api]"
+
+# 4. Start the API
+rule-engine-api                    # http://127.0.0.1:8000  (docs at /docs)
+```
+
+> If activation is blocked with a script-execution error, run once:
+> `Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass` then retry step 2.
+> On **cmd.exe** instead of PowerShell, activate with `.venv\Scripts\activate.bat`.
+
+Console commands available after install: `rule-engine`, `rule-engine-single`,
+`rule-engine-api`.
+
+### Frontend (analyst review UI)
+
+Open a **second** terminal in the project root and run:
+
+```powershell
+# 1. Confirm the right Node
+node --version                     # -> v20.9.x
+
+# 2. Install and start the UI (points at the API on :8000 via ui\.env.local)
+cd ui
+npm install
+npm run dev                        # http://localhost:3000
+```
+
+Keep the backend (`rule-engine-api`) running in the first terminal, then open
+http://localhost:3000, edit the payload, and click **Evaluate**.
+
+### macOS / Linux
+
+Same steps; the only differences are venv activation
+(`source .venv/bin/activate`) and setting env vars with `export` instead of the
+PowerShell/`set` syntax.
+
+## REST API
+
+```powershell
+# Windows (PowerShell): optional rules dir override, default is "rules"
+$env:RULE_ENGINE_RULES_DIR = "rules"
+rule-engine-api                      # serves on http://127.0.0.1:8000
+# or: uvicorn rule_engine.api.app:app --reload
+```
+
+```bash
+# macOS / Linux
+export RULE_ENGINE_RULES_DIR=rules
+rule-engine-api
+```
+
+| Method | Path         | Purpose                                   |
+|--------|--------------|-------------------------------------------|
+| GET    | `/health`    | Liveness + discovered rule sets           |
+| GET    | `/rule-sets` | List available exception codes            |
+| POST   | `/evaluate`  | Evaluate `{exception_codes, documents, application}` |
+
+Interactive docs (typed request/response schema for the UI) at `/docs`.
+
+Request/response models live in `src/rule_engine/api/models.py`. The response
+gives the UI: an overall `decision`, per-code `summary` counts (for badges),
+`blocking_failures`, a flat `executed_rules` list (for tables), and a nested
+`result_tree` (for the AND/OR hierarchy).
+
+## Run
+
+```bash
+# Service: a request payload ({exception_codes, documents, application})
+rule-engine data/request_payload.json
+rule-engine data/request_payload.json --json
+
+# Single rule set against a single input file
+rule-engine-single rules/SOF002_VERIFICATION.json data/sample_input.json
+
+# Same, without installing (source-checkout shims)
+python3 run_service.py data/request_payload.json
+python3 main.py rules/SOF002_VERIFICATION.json data/sample_input.json
+```
+
+## Use as a library
+
+```python
+from rule_engine import RuleEngineService
+
+service = RuleEngineService.from_directory("rules")
+result = service.evaluate(
+    exception_codes=["SOF002"],
+    documents={"INVOICE": {"counterparty_name": "Acme", ...}},
+    application={"currency": "GBP", ...},
+)
+
+result.decision                              # overall pass/fail
+result.reports["SOF002"].decision            # per-code decision
+result.reports["SOF002"].executed_rules      # audit trail of every rule run
+result.to_dict()                             # fully serialisable payload
+```
+
+## Core concepts
+
+### Rule tree
+A node is either a **group** or a **leaf**.
+
+- **Group**: has `logical_operator` (`AND` / `OR` / `NOT`) and a nested `rules` list.
+- **Leaf**: has an `operator`, a `left_operand`, an optional `right_operand`,
+  a `severity` and a `failure_reason`.
+
+The tree is evaluated **depth-first**, and every node is evaluated — there is no
+short-circuiting, so the audit trail is always complete.
+
+### Gated groups (conditional branches)
+A group may carry a condition of its own — an `operator` with a `left_operand`
+and optional `right_operand`, sitting alongside `logical_operator`. The nested
+rules run only if that condition holds; otherwise the whole subtree is skipped.
+
+```json
+{
+  "rule_id": "GATE010",
+  "description": "US tax checks (only for USD invoices)",
+  "operator": "equals",
+  "left_operand": { "source": "DOCUMENT", "document_type": "INVOICE", "field": "currency" },
+  "right_operand": { "source": "STATIC", "value": "USD" },
+  "failure_reason": "Invoice is not in USD, US tax checks do not apply",
+  "on_condition_fail": "SKIP",
+  "logical_operator": "AND",
+  "rules": [ "...nested rules, only evaluated when the condition holds..." ]
+}
+```
+
+`on_condition_fail` decides what an unmet condition means. Either way the nested
+rules are skipped:
+
+- **`SKIP`** (default) — the branch is *not applicable*. It stays neutral in the
+  enclosing `AND` / `OR` / `NOT` and cannot block the decision. A group whose
+  every child was skipped becomes skipped itself, so the neutrality propagates.
+- **`FAIL`** — the unmet condition *is* the failure. The group fails and the
+  condition is reported as the blocking failure (defaulting to `CRITICAL`
+  severity, so it blocks under `ALL_CRITICAL_MUST_PASS` unless you set a lower
+  `severity` on the group).
+
+A condition that could not be evaluated at all (unknown operator, malformed
+operand) always blocks, whatever the policy — a gate that never ran must not
+silently swallow a branch.
+
+Skipped rules stay in the report with outcome `SKIPPED` and a reason naming the
+gate that shut, so a reviewer can see what was *not* checked and why. The gate
+itself appears in `executed_rules` marked `is_group_condition`.
+
+`rules/GATED_GROUP_DEMO.json` + `data/gated_demo_input.json` are a worked example
+covering every operator, source, logical operator, severity and gate policy —
+including gates that open, gates that shut, sibling gates routing on
+`scenario_type`, a nested gate whose condition never runs, and a group that
+becomes skipped because all of its children were:
+
+```bash
+rule-engine-single rules/GATED_GROUP_DEMO.json data/gated_demo_input.json
+```
+
+### Operands & sources
+Each operand declares a `source`; the engine resolves it to a value:
+
+| source        | resolves from                                  |
+|---------------|------------------------------------------------|
+| `STATIC`      | the literal `value` in the operand             |
+| `DOCUMENT`    | `documents[document_type][field]`              |
+| `DOCUMENT`    | `documents[document_type]` — the whole document, when `field` is omitted |
+| `APPLICATION` | `application[field]`                           |
+
+Missing document/application fields resolve to `None` (treated as empty) so a
+missing value simply fails a `not_empty` check instead of crashing.
+
+`STATIC` is a **literal**, never a field name. To test an application field,
+point the operand at it — `{"source": "APPLICATION", "field": "scenario_type"}`
+— rather than putting `"scenario_type"` in a `STATIC` value, which would compare
+the string `"scenario_type"` itself.
+
+Omitting `field` from a `DOCUMENT` operand resolves the whole document, which is
+how you ask whether a document was supplied at all:
+
+```json
+{ "operator": "is_present",
+  "left_operand": { "source": "DOCUMENT", "document_type": "INVOICE" } }
+```
+
+### Operators
+`not_empty`, `is_empty`, `is_present`, `not_present`, `equals`, `not_equals`,
+`contains`, `not_contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `greater_than`,
+`less_than`, `greater_than_or_equal`, `less_than_or_equal`, `before`, `after`.
+
+`is_empty` / `not_empty` / `is_present` / `not_present` are unary — they ignore
+the right operand.
+
+Presence and emptiness are different questions. `is_present` asks whether the
+operand resolved to anything at all; `not_empty` asks whether what it resolved
+to has content. A document that was supplied but carries no fields is *present*
+and *empty*. An absent document, an absent field and an explicit `null` all
+count as not present.
+
+Add your own with the `@operator("name")` decorator in `operators.py`.
+
+### Evaluation modes
+The tree produces a pure boolean (`logical_passed`), but the final `decision`
+depends on `evaluation_mode`:
+
+- **`ALL_CRITICAL_MUST_PASS`** — passes as long as no `CRITICAL` leaf failed;
+  lower-severity failures are recorded as warnings but do not block.
+- **`STRICT`** — the whole boolean tree must be `True`.
+
+Skipped branches count in neither mode: a rule that never ran can neither
+satisfy a check nor breach one.
+
+Add modes in `EVALUATION_MODES` in `engine.py`.
+
+## Low-level API (single rule set)
+
+For running one rule-set dict directly, bypassing the repository/service:
+
+```python
+from rule_engine import RuleEngine, EvaluationContext
+import json
+
+rules = json.load(open("rules/SOF002_VERIFICATION.json"))
+ctx = EvaluationContext(
+    documents={"INVOICE": {"counterparty_name": "Acme", "counterparty_address": ""}},
+    application={"requested_amount": 15000},
+)
+report = RuleEngine(rules).evaluate(ctx)
+
+print(report.decision)            # final pass/fail (after evaluation_mode)
+print(report.logical_passed)      # raw boolean of the whole tree
+print(report.blocking_failures)   # leaves that caused the decision to FAIL
+print(report.executed_rules)      # audit trail of every rule that ran
+print(report.to_dict())           # full serialisable report
+```
