+A **checklist runner**. You define checks (**rules**), send in some data, and it
+tells you what passed, what failed, and whether the overall result is pass/fail.
+
+- A **rule** = one check, e.g. *"invoice counterparty name must not be empty"*.
+- Rules are grouped with **AND** ("all must pass") or **OR** ("any can pass").
+- Each rule has a **severity**. `CRITICAL` can block the result; lower ones that
+  fail are just **warnings**.
+- Data comes from **documents** (extracted fields) and **application** (entered
+  data).
+- You ask by **exception code** (e.g. `SOF002`); each code has its own rules.
+
+### Example
+
+Check: *invoice counterparty name must not be empty* (CRITICAL).
+
+```json
+{
+  "rule_id": "RULE001",
+  "operator": "not_empty",
+  "left_operand": { "source": "DOCUMENT", "document_type": "INVOICE", "field": "counterparty_name" },
+  "severity": "CRITICAL",
+  "failure_reason": "Counterparty name missing"
+}
+```
+
+Data sent in:
+
+```json
+{
+  "exception_codes": ["SOF002"],
+  "documents": { "INVOICE": { "counterparty_name": "Acme Trading Ltd" } },
+  "application": {}
+}
+```
+
+Result: name is present → **PASS**. If it were empty or missing → **FAIL**
+(critical), with reason `Counterparty name missing (INVOICE.counterparty_name='')`.
+
+To compare two fields, point the operands at different sources — e.g.
+`equals` with left = `DOCUMENT INVOICE.invoice_amount` and right =
+`APPLICATION requested_amount`.
+
+---
+
+## Prompt
+
+Build a **dependency-free Python library** `rule-engine` that runs a tree of
+rules against document and application data.
+
+**Rules (JSON).** A file has `rule_set_id`, `exception_code`, `evaluation_mode`,
+and `rules`. A node is either a **group** (`logical_operator` AND/OR/NOT +
+nested `rules`) or a **leaf** (`operator`, `left_operand`, `right_operand`,
+`severity`, `failure_reason`). IDs are `RULE001`, `RULE002`, … in order.
+
+**Operands.** Each has a `source`: `STATIC` (literal `value`), `DOCUMENT`
+(`documents[document_type][field]`), or `APPLICATION` (`application[field]`).
+Missing fields resolve to `None` (never crash).
+
+**Operators** (registry-based, easy to add): `not_empty`, `is_empty`, `equals`,
+`not_equals`, `contains`, `not_contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`,
+`greater_than`, `less_than`, `greater_than_or_equal`, `less_than_or_equal`,
+`before`, `after`. `is_empty`/`not_empty` are unary.
+
+**Normalization.** String compares trim whitespace + ignore case. Date compares
+(`before`/`after`) parse many formats (ISO first, then common ones; numeric
+dates are day-first/UK). Keep the original values in output — normalize only for
+the comparison.
+
+**Two results.** `logical_passed` = raw true/false of the whole tree.
+`decision` = verdict after `evaluation_mode`:
+- `ALL_CRITICAL_MUST_PASS` — fails only if a CRITICAL check blocks; others are
+  warnings.
+- `STRICT` — the whole tree must be true.
+
+Decision is **context-aware**: a failing CRITICAL leaf only blocks if it truly
+causes the failure (not if an OR sibling passes or a NOT rescues it). Report
+`blocking_failures` = the leaves actually responsible.
+
+**Output.** Per rule set: `decision`, `logical_passed`, `blocking_failures`, and
+`executed_rules` (flat list — each with rule_id, description, operator,
+severity, left/right operand location + value, passed, outcome
+[PASS/FAIL/WARN/ERROR], reason). Reasons name the operand + value, e.g.
+`(INVOICE.currency='USD' vs application.currency='GBP')`.
+
+**Repository + service.** `RuleRepository.from_directory(dir)` indexes rule sets
+by `exception_code`. `RuleEngineService.evaluate(exception_codes, documents,
+application)` runs the matching sets and returns per-code results + an overall
+`decision` + `unknown_codes`.
+
+**Packaging.** `src/` layout, `pyproject.toml`, version `0.1.0`, zero core deps.
+CLIs: `rule-engine` (payload), `rule-engine-single` (one file). Add `.gitignore`.
+
+**REST API (optional `[api]` extra: fastapi/uvicorn/pydantic).** Sub-package
+`rule_engine.api` with `POST /evaluate`, `GET /rule-sets`, `GET /health`, Swagger
+at `/docs`. Request = `{exception_codes, documents, application}`. Response =
+overall `decision`, `unknown_codes`, and per code a `summary` (counts),
+`blocking_failures`, `executed_rules`, and nested `result_tree`.
+
+**Deliverables.** The package, sample rules in `rules/` (a SOF002 set + a demo
+using all operators), sample inputs in `data/`, and a `README.md`.
